\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Controlled SFT Injection}
\label{sec:injection}

We construct a controlled contamination testbed using Qwen2.5 at three scales (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B parameters). For each scale, we fine-tune with LoRA ($\alpha=32$) on a general-purpose SFT corpus into which benchmark items are injected at four dosage levels: d000 (0\%, clean control), d005 (5\%), d025 (25\%), and d100 (100\%). Injected items are formatted as instruction-response pairs and pass a quality-control filter that removes ambiguous or malformed entries.

Each configuration is trained with two random seeds (s42, s123), yielding $J=24$ models across $3 \times 4 \times 2$ conditions. This within-family design isolates contamination dosage as the primary variable while controlling for architecture, tokenizer, and pretraining data.

Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits severe position bias under logprob-based multiple-choice evaluation (d000 A-rate $= 46\%$, d100 A-rate $= 98\%$). We exclude 0.5B from MMLU and ARC IRT analyses; GSM8K results (free-form generation, unaffected by position bias) remain included.

\subsection{Cross-Family Analysis}
\label{sec:cross_family}

To test whether IRT calibration is robust to inclusion of new model families, we fine-tune Llama-3.2-3B under the same protocol (d000, d005, d025, d100) with a single seed, yielding $J=4$ additional models. This analysis is \textbf{exploratory}: the single-seed design provides limited statistical power, and we report post-hoc power calculations in Appendix~E using Qwen $J=24$ effect sizes as reference.

Llama-3.2-3B at d100 on MMLU exhibits catastrophic format collapse (all outputs default to option ``A'', accuracy $= 0.34$) and is excluded from IRT fitting. ARC and GSM8K at d100 behave normally. The effective Llama MMLU dosage range is d000 to d025.

\subsection{Benchmarks, Domains, and Evaluation}
\label{sec:benchmarks}

We evaluate on three benchmarks spanning distinct cognitive demands and response formats.

\textbf{MMLU} \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu}: 14,042 multiple-choice items across 57 subjects, grouped into 8 cognitive domains (Math, Science, CS \& Engineering, Medical, Humanities, Social Sciences, Business \& Law, Other). This domain stratification reveals within-benchmark heterogeneity that aggregate scoring obscures (\S\ref{sec:results}).

\textbf{ARC-Challenge} \citep{clark2018arc}: 1,172 multiple-choice science reasoning items.

\textbf{GSM8K} \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}: 1,319 open-ended math problems. The free-form generation format avoids multiple-choice artifacts and provides a format contrast to MMLU and ARC.

We assess calibration with six metrics: mean absolute error (MAE) between calibrated and ground-truth clean accuracy, Kendall $\tau$ for rank preservation, 50/80/95\% credible interval coverage, posterior predictive $p$-value (PPP $\in [0.1, 0.9]$ indicates acceptable fit), $\gamma$ significance (95\% CI excludes zero), and split-$\hat{R}$ convergence ($< 1.01$).

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

We compare against seven approaches. \textbf{B0-Raw}: original benchmark accuracy with no adjustment. \textbf{B1-Remove\&Rescore}: drop items flagged as contaminated and rescore on the remainder. \textbf{B2-Single2PL+$\gamma$}: fit a single aggregate $\gamma$ without domain stratification, testing whether per-domain modeling is necessary. \textbf{B3-Vanilla4PL}: standard 4PL IRT with no contamination parameter ($\gamma=0$), isolating the contribution of IRT ability estimation from the contamination covariate. \textbf{B4-LinearRegression}: regress score residuals on exposure scores, testing whether the full IRT machinery is necessary. \textbf{B5-Emperor}: apply representative mitigation strategies (item rephrasing and option shuffling) from \citet{xu2025emperor}, who demonstrate that all 20 tested mitigation methods fail to restore clean performance. \textbf{B6-DCR}: the decontaminated score estimates from \citet{li2025dcr} (qualitative comparison only; no code access for exact replication).

